  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...High pressure builds in from the west through \nmost of this week with sunny skies. Building hts as upper ridge\nstrengthens sends temps soaring through the 80s and nearing 90\nfor some interior locales (record high climate section below). \nA back door front moves into nrn parts of ENC later Thur, but \nquickly retreats north by early Friday with another surge of \nswrly flow ahead of much stronger front. No moisture with the \nbackdoor front, and only temporary reductions in temps for nrn \n1/3 of ENC. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...The aforementioned much stronger cold front will\nsweep through ENC Friday night, and good model agreement with\nthis system. Front still appears moisture stared due to it's\nvery fast movement and no connection to the Gulf. Strong forcing\nis associated with it however, so a skinny line of showers may\ndrop only a few hundredths at best for most areas in the span of\nless than an hour Fri evening. Coolest and driest airmass \nfor the fall season thus far builds in behind this front with\ntemps taking a nosedive with highs in the 60s and lows in the\n40s (inland) and 50s (beaches). \n\nWith this strong front, winds rapidly inc from the NW Fri \nevening with a short period of gales for most of the waters and \nsounds, and would bring a sharp increase in high seas especially\non Sat as the winds turn northerly and persist to near gales on\nthe coastal waters. Minor coastal impacts could occur too, \nthough short duration of strongest winds may act to limit more \nappreciable impacts. It's still too soon for specifics on \ncoastal flooding potential.",
